## 3. Analytical Grounding: Saint-Venant at Aspect Ratio b/p ≈ 0.9740

### 3.1 The 1855 Analytical Apparatus

Saint-Venant torsion theory, developed in 1855 by Adhémar Jean Claude Barré de Saint-Venant, provides the analytical framework for calculating the torsional stiffness coefficient β of a rectangular bar under applied torque. The theory has been peer-reviewed continuously since publication and appears in every mechanical engineering textbook covering torsion mechanics (Timoshenko, Boresi and Schmidt, Roark's Formulas for Stress and Strain, and standard engineering tables).

The Saint-Venant β coefficient for a rectangular cross-section with long side b and short side p is:

```
β(b, p) = (1/3) · [1 − (192 / π⁵) · (p/b) · Σ_{n=1,3,5,...} (1/n⁵) · tanh(nπb / (2p))]
```

The infinite series converges rapidly because of the n⁵ denominator; the first term (n = 1) accounts for greater than 99% of the total value. Standard engineering reference values include β(b/p = 1.0) = 0.140577 (perfect square cross-section), β(b/p = 2.0) = 0.228682, and β(b/p → ∞) = 1/3 (thin plate limit).

The physical intuition behind β is load-bearing for understanding what the Saint-Venant grounding demonstrates. When a rectangular bar is twisted, the shear stress cannot distribute uniformly across the cross-section. The corners are a specific geometric problem: a corner is where two free surfaces meet at a right angle, and shear stress at a free surface can only run parallel to that surface. At the corner point itself, the material would have to simultaneously carry shear parallel to two perpendicular free surfaces, which is geometrically impossible. The shear stress at the corner is therefore exactly zero — not "small," not "approximately zero," but zero as a boundary condition of the geometry itself. The load redistributes: the corners carry nothing, the interior carries more to compensate, and the middle of each long edge carries maximum shear. This is the "corner shear-stress-zero boundary condition" referenced in standard engineering torsion tables. It is not a correction factor applied to the analysis. It is what the analysis honestly accounts for when computing β under substrate-honest boundary conditions — the corners cannot participate in carrying the torsional load, so the effective load-carrying cross-section is smaller than the full geometric square.

