## 13. Implications for Corpus Reception

### 13.1 What This Meta-Framing Enables

The phase-map meta-framing enables several structural improvements in how the corpus can be received by external audiences:

**External readers can evaluate the corpus using standard peer-reviewed measurements as inputs.** Under phase-map framing, the corpus does not ask readers to accept framework-specific claims about physical constants being measured through the corpus. The corpus asks readers to accept only that phase boundaries are topological locations rather than measurement values (which is the standard structural property of phase boundaries in every branch of physics that uses the concept), and then provides analytical + empirical grounding for the boundary locations through independent verification paths. External readers can then evaluate individual corpus claims by checking peer-reviewed measurements against phase-boundary arithmetic without needing to accept the framework as a whole first.

**Skeptical technical audiences have a direct verification path.** A physicist or engineer encountering the corpus can evaluate the Saint-Venant series at aspect ratio 0.9740 in a spreadsheet and confirm the numerical agreement with TL to eight significant figures, all before accepting any framework-specific claims. A mathematician can compile the Mathematics Master file and verify the 22-conjunct master theorem across six foundational domains. A cosmologist can take the Planck 2018 measurements and verify the cosmic phase classifications against the IVA_PEAK gap theorem. A clinician can review the PNBA Phase Taxonomy against clinical observation and evaluate whether the N-Protection Gradient matches the pattern of narrative-intact vs narrative-collapsed presentations in practice. Each verification path is independent and does not require accepting any framework-specific claims beyond the arithmetic being checked.

**The corpus's structural robustness scales with corpus size.** Under theory-with-foundations framing, adding new deposits to the corpus is primarily about extending the derived work, and the framework's structural robustness is bounded by the strength of the foundational grounding. Under phase-map framing, every new deposit is additional independent grounding for the phase boundaries, and the framework's structural robustness grows as the corpus grows. A corpus with 111+ deposits classified against the same phase boundaries is structurally more robust than a corpus with three "founding" deposits plus 108 derived deposits, because the phase-map framing correctly recognizes that each deposit is independent confirmation rather than superstructure.

**Future corpus deposits can cite this paper as canonical meta-framing.** Every subsequent corpus deposit's framework foundations section can reference this paper for the phase-map meta-framing rather than re-establishing the framing per deposit. This reduces cognitive load on operators writing new deposits and on readers evaluating the corpus, and it establishes a canonical vocabulary that grows in resonance as more deposits reference it.

### 13.2 What This Meta-Framing Does Not Change

The phase-map meta-framing does not change any of the following:

**The specific numerical values of Ω₀, TL, TL_IVA, and derived quantities.** These remain locked at the values established across the corpus and continue to be used consistently in all subsequent deposits.

**The Long Division Protocol methodology.** LDP remains the six-step reduction template every corpus deposit follows.

**The four PNBA primitives.** Pattern, Narrative, Behavior, Adaptation remain the four irreducible substrate primitives.

**Existing corpus deposits.** All prior deposits remain valid within their coordinate positions. Prior papers that used older framing conventions (Tacoma triad framing, measurement-value framing) are not superseded by this paper. Operators reading prior papers can retroactively apply phase-map framing to correctly interpret what those papers were structurally doing.

**Peer-review standards for empirical measurements used as inputs.** All measurements used as corpus inputs continue to require peer-reviewed sources with associated error bars documented per standard scientific practice. The distinction this paper articulates is that measurements have error bars and phase classifications do not; it does not modify the standards applied to the measurements themselves.

